WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.218 --> 00:00:01.010
ROGER MARTINEZ: Hi.

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:02.520
Roger here from Google Cloud.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.800
And in this video, we'll
have a look at Pay Later

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.690
and its relation
to Open Banking,

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.760
a sample implementation
of the API,

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.080
and the wider picture of
consuming and publishing APIs.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.020
Open Banking is a system of
allowing access and control

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.970
of consumer banking
and financial accounts

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.120
through third
party applications.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.420
It encourages interoperability
between banks and consumers

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.460
by making available the
bank's core services,

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.100
and accelerating their
digital transformation.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.140
First, we'll show you
a common use case,

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.130
and then we'll
unpack it for you.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.520
We'll start with the
end-user experience.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.960
An API consumer, in
this case a merchant

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.410
in the e-commerce industry
offering last-minute Chromebook

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.480
deals, develops a
new app experience

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.980
using Google Assistant.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.450
Let's have a look.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.247
Talk to Pixelbook Deals Store.

00:00:49.247 --> 00:00:49.830
SPEAKER 2: Hi.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.620
Welcome to the
Pixelbook Deals Store.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.870
What can I do for you?

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.320
ROGER MARTINEZ: Are there
any special offerings?

00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.492
SPEAKER 2: Here you go.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.230
You've selected Pixelbook
Go, 16 gigabytes RAM,

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:08.740
costing 854 pounds.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.500
Would you like to
pay now or pay later?

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.987
ROGER MARTINEZ: Pay later.

00:01:12.987 --> 00:01:13.570
SPEAKER 2: OK.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:16.880
Now choose the bank you're with.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.880
After login to your bank, I need
to link your Pixelbook Deals

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.850
Store account to Google.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.560
Is that OK?

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:24.715
ROGER MARTINEZ: Yes.

00:01:24.715 --> 00:01:26.340
When your account
was linked to Google,

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.060
the app can customize
your experience

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.800
based on your identity.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.680
Financial data or authentication
data cannot be collected via

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.440
the conversational interface.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.500
So we're now shown the terms
and conditions for Pay Later,

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.740
and there's a link to
review those conditions.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.390
Simply click on
it and then return

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.070
to the deal page when ready.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.240
Here's a sample reference to
the terms and conditions page.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.620
Whenever we're ready, we can
go back to the main screen

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.120
and say, all good.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.933
We now see some of the
loan offers to Pay Later.

00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:56.850
We can scroll up and
still reference the terms

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.680
and conditions if we want to.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.040
Once we're ready, we'll
select the desired loan.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.400
SPEAKER 2: OK that's it.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.266
Pixelbook Go, 16 gigabytes
RAM is now yours.

00:02:05.266 --> 00:02:08.220
We'll take it from there and
send you a confirmation email

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.660
across shortly.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:10.530
Speak soon.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.445
Bye.

00:02:12.445 --> 00:02:14.070
ROGER MARTINEZ: Until
now, we've looked

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:15.820
at this from the
perspective of a merchant

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.390
leveraging the bank's Pay Later
APIs to improve the checkout

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:19.460
experience.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.810
But how do we get access
in the first place?

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.970
Stepping back to look
at the wider picture,

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.400
there are more actors involved
to get it all working.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.020
First, the bank wants
to share a service.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.620
In this case, the Pay Later
API, but still maintain

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.440
some restrictions, such
as applying a call limit.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.920
Second, it's the merchant who is
asking for access to those APIs

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.020
in order to build better apps.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.410
Third, it's API product managers
within the organization who

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.080
treat APIs as products,
and therefore are

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.480
looking at publishing
easy onboarding content

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.900
and getting an overview
of the usage statistics.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.870
Now we'll have a look at how a
developer can find those APIs.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.630
In this case, as an
API consumer again.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.670
Here's a sample developer
portal of the bank,

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.590
which allows anyone to browse
through the API catalog.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:06.080
Here we are already logged
in as an app developer

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.103
of a partnering organization,
in this case, the merchant.

00:03:10.103 --> 00:03:12.020
And the first thing a
developer typically does

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.450
is build a developer
application.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.460
We already have here the
Deal Finder Assistant,

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.470
previously created.

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:21.110
Upon approval of the bank,
you receive credentials.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:26.030
Namely an API key and secret
to access this Pay Later API.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:28.730
Very often, developers
want to test this API

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:30.590
and want to see what
the response is.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.810
Or how to embed it
in the application.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.390
We can go into the Pay
Later API specification,

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:39.230
and we can see all the
different paths and operations.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:46.310
Under Loan, Payment, and Refund,
we can send a test request.

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:49.430
For that, first of all,
we have to authorize.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:52.100
We select the app from before
and authorize that the API

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.050
key credentials be stored.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:59.750
Now we can enter some consumer
ID and some payment resource

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.390
identification, and
execute the call.

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:05.240
This will return a payload, and
give a developer an overview

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.290
of how the API works.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.950
As you can see, Apigee helps
accelerate the publishing

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:12.140
of APIs, making them
ready for consumption

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:15.380
while keeping an eye on
where they're being used.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:18.260
All in all, this shows what's
possible beyond Open Banking.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.440
Namely, the opportunity to build
new customer channels with more

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.050
control over the data,
developers, and partners

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.090
to create a
connected experience.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.850
This enhances interoperability
between traditional banking

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.460
data with an API ecosystem.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:32.840
Thanks for listening
in, and feel

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.940
free to read more about
Open Banking solution

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.880
and what Apigee provides for
APIs in financial services

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.870
at the link listed in
the description below.

